    "source": "lecture_05.pdf",
    "page": 122,
    "text": "STAT GR 5293 Sec002 | Spring 2026\nQuantitative performance metrics for\nLLM Inference\n•\nPerformance metrics for LLM inference\n•\nCompleted requests per minute\n•\nTime to first token (TTFT): how long before the LLM returns the first token\n•\nInter-token latency (ITL): average time between consecutive tokens\n•\nEnd-to-end Latency: approximately the same as the average output length of tokens multiplied\nby the inter-token latency\n•\nCost per typical request: API providers can usually trade off one of the other metrics for cost.\nFor example, you can reduce latency by running the same model on more GPUs or using higher-\nend GPUs.\nhttps://www.anyscale.com/blog/reproducible-performance-metrics-for-llm-inference",
    "method": "native",
    "native_text_length": 707
  },
  {
    "source": "lecture_05.pdf",
    "page": 123,
    "text": "STAT GR 5293 Sec002 | Spring 2026\nMetrics for LLM Reasoning\n•\nLarge language models show improved downstream task performance\nwhen prompted to generate step-by-step reasoning to justify their final\nanswers (Chain of Thought, Tree of Thought, ReAct)\n•\nReasoning steps greatly improve model interpretability and verification,\n•\nObjectively studying the correctness of reasoning is difficult\n•\nHow often the stated reasoning steps actually support the final end task\npredictions?",
    "method": "native",
    "native_text_length": 476
  },
  {
    "source": "lecture_05.pdf",
    "page": 124,
    "text": "STAT GR 5293 Sec002 | Spring 2026\nExample of 2-shot reasoning\n2-shot examples\nStep-by-step reasoning for logical inference\ntask generated from 2-shot GPT-3",
    "method": "native",
    "native_text_length": 155
  },
